REV.page({"course":"python","id":"w09","title":"Object-oriented programming: classes, objects and inheritance","summary":"How Python models real things. An object keeps its own data (attributes) together with its own behaviour (methods), and a class is the blueprint that every object of one kind follows. Inheritance then lets a child class reuse, extend or replace what its parent class has, so nothing is written twice.","n":9,"lectures":[{"code":"9.1","title":"Introduction to OOP | object-oriented programming basics","video":"X1mOrCwZUgQ","seconds":245},{"code":"9.2","title":"Classes & objects | object oriented programming fundamentals explained","video":"ZPtRsQrZLcE","seconds":1024},{"code":"9.3","title":"Attributes & methods | OOP, init, self, and behavior explained","video":"n_jPQlWhGhM","seconds":1371},{"code":"9.4","title":"Inheritance & method overriding | types of inheritance & file importing","video":"RrFvcDjJptY","seconds":1896}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"why-objects-attributes-and-behaviour","level":2,"title":"Why objects: attributes and behaviour"},{"id":"classes-and-objects","level":2,"title":"Classes and objects"},{"id":"one-blueprint-many-houses","level":3,"title":"One blueprint, many houses"},{"id":"your-first-class","level":3,"title":"Your first class"},{"id":"making-an-object-the-constructor","level":3,"title":"Making an object: the constructor"},{"id":"the-dot-operator","level":3,"title":"The dot operator"},{"id":"each-object-keeps-its-own-values","level":3,"title":"Each object keeps its own values"},{"id":"the-proper-way-init-and-self","level":2,"title":"The proper way: __init__ and self"},{"id":"init-runs-by-itself","level":3,"title":"__init__ runs by itself"},{"id":"self-is-the-current-object","level":3,"title":"self is the current object"},{"id":"self-roll-no-roll-no-two-different-variables","level":3,"title":"self.roll_no = roll_no: two different variables"},{"id":"class-attributes-and-object-attributes","level":2,"title":"Class attributes and object attributes"},{"id":"behaviour-methods","level":2,"title":"Behaviour: methods"},{"id":"pass-or-fail-the-result-method","level":3,"title":"Pass or fail: the result method"},{"id":"function-or-method","level":3,"title":"Function or method?"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-homework-int-str-list-and-dict","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s homework: int, str, list and dict"},{"id":"inheritance","level":2,"title":"Inheritance"},{"id":"parents-and-children","level":3,"title":"Parents and children"},{"id":"two-classes-that-repeat-themselves","level":3,"title":"Two classes that repeat themselves"},{"id":"a-parent-class-person","level":3,"title":"A parent class: Person"},{"id":"super-run-the-parent-s-code","level":3,"title":"super(): run the parent’s code"},{"id":"method-overriding","level":2,"title":"Method overriding"},{"id":"one-class-per-file-import","level":2,"title":"One class per file: import"},{"id":"private-members","level":2,"title":"Private members"},{"id":"what-the-two-underscores-really-do","level":3,"title":"What the two underscores really do"},{"id":"types-of-inheritance","level":2,"title":"Types of inheritance"},{"id":"oop-mistakes-and-their-messages","level":2,"title":"OOP mistakes and their messages"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Objects</strong> (9.1). Every real thing has <strong>attributes</strong> (a student’s roll number, name, marks) and <strong>behaviour</strong> (what it does, such as passing or failing). <strong>Object-oriented programming</strong> (OOP) keeps both together in one <strong>object</strong> per student, so that each student has an identity of their own.</li>\n<li><strong>Class and object</strong> (9.2). A <strong>class</strong> is a blueprint; an <strong>object</strong> is one house built from it. <code>class Student:</code> writes the blueprint, <code>s0 = Student()</code> calls the <strong>constructor</strong> to build an object, and <code>s0.name</code> uses the <strong>dot operator</strong> to reach what belongs to <code>s0</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>__init__</code> and <code>self</code></strong> (9.3). <code>Student(<wbr>0, 'Bhuvanesh')</code> runs the method <code>__init__</code> by itself. Inside it, <code>self</code> is the object being built, so <code>self.<wbr>roll_no = roll_no</code> stores the value inside that object.</li>\n<li><strong>Class attribute or object attribute</strong> (9.3). A variable written directly in the class (<code>count = 0</code>) has one copy, shared by all objects: use it as <code>Student.<wbr>count</code>. A variable set through <code>self</code> belongs to each object separately.</li>\n<li><strong>Methods</strong> (9.3). A function inside a class is a <strong>method</strong>. <code>result()</code> prints <code>Pass</code> or <code>Fail</code> from the object’s own total: that is the student’s behaviour. <code>int</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>list</code> and <code>dict</code> are classes too.</li>\n<li><strong>Inheritance</strong> (9.4). What <code>Student</code> and <code>Employee</code> share (name and age) moves into a <strong>parent class</strong> <code>Person</code>. <code>class Student(<wbr>Person):</code> inherits it, and <code>super().<wbr>__init__(<wbr>name, age)</code> and <code>super().<wbr>display()</code> run the parent’s code. Every student <strong>is a</strong> person.</li>\n<li><strong>Overriding, files, privacy</strong> (9.4). A child’s own <code>display</code> <strong>overrides</strong> (replaces) the parent’s. Each class can live in its own file, joined with <code>from Person import Person</code>. Two underscores, as in <code>self.__age</code>, make a <strong>private member</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Five types of inheritance</strong> (9.4): simple, hierarchical, multiple, multilevel and hybrid.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"why-objects-attributes-and-behaviour\">Why objects: attributes and behaviour</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 9.1, Introduction to OOP.</em> The video shows only its title slide; everything is in the narration.</p>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>So far your programs have had <strong>variables</strong>, which store values, and <strong>functions</strong>, which work on them (<a href=\"#/python/w05\">Week 5</a>). Now think of real things. You have a name, an age and a gender, and you can sing, dance or play a sport. Your friends, your family members and your laptop are also things with their own facts and their own actions. The lecture calls each such thing, living or non-living, an <strong>object</strong>: “I am an object, you are an object, ... this laptop is an object” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1mOrCwZUgQ&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>. <strong>Object-oriented programming</strong> puts these objects at the centre of the program.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>This week is about a new approach to programming, <strong>object-oriented programming</strong>, popularly called <strong>OOP</strong>. A <strong>paradigm</strong> is a style of writing programs. The lecture calls OOP the most popular programming paradigm, “the standard way to write the code” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1mOrCwZUgQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>.</p>\n<p>Every object has two kinds of things, attributes and behaviour <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1mOrCwZUgQ&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>:</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Attribute, behaviour and object</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>attribute</strong> is a fact about an object: your name, your age, your gender. Attributes are stored in <strong>variables</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Behaviour</strong> is what an object does: singing, dancing, sports. Behaviour is written as <strong>functions</strong>.</li>\n<li>An <strong>object</strong> is one entity that holds its <strong>own</strong> attributes and its <strong>own</strong> behaviour together.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s familiar example is the <strong>scores dataset</strong> of Computational Thinking: 30 students, and for each one a roll number, name, gender, city, date of birth, marks in three subjects, and the total <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1mOrCwZUgQ&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>. These attributes are easy to store in variables, and we can write functions that calculate with them: “we all have been doing this day in day out” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1mOrCwZUgQ&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Object</th>\n<th>Attributes</th>\n<th>Behaviour</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>You</td>\n<td>name, age, gender</td>\n<td>singing, dancing, sports</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A student</td>\n<td>roll number, name, gender, city, date of birth, three marks, total</td>\n<td>calculations with these values (in 9.3: pass or fail)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>The problem</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1mOrCwZUgQ&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>. In the programs so far, the same variables and functions are <strong>shared</strong> by all 30 students. The real world does not work like that. Every student has their own identity, made of their own attributes and behaviour, so every student should have their <strong>own</strong> variables and functions, not shared with other students <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1mOrCwZUgQ&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>The answer</strong> is an entity that stores variables and functions together, one for every student: the <strong>object</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1mOrCwZUgQ&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>. This is why OOP is so popular: “it translates the real world into programming”. The next lectures show how Python does it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"classes-and-objects\">Classes and objects</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 9.2, Classes and objects.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"one-blueprint-many-houses\">One blueprint, many houses</h3>\n<p>A recap first <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>: attributes are variables, behaviour is functions, and an object combines both. If every student has a fully separate set of variables and functions, a new problem appears: there is no way to keep <strong>uniformity</strong>, that is, to make every student follow the same pattern <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>. The lecture’s example <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the first student has all the attributes of the scores dataset;</li>\n<li>the second student has all of them except the date of birth: one attribute is <strong>missing</strong>;</li>\n<li>the third student has one <strong>extra</strong> attribute, an email address.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Missing or extra attributes cause a lot of problems when we start computing with the data <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>. So we want every student to keep their own identity, but to express it through the same, predefined attributes, like the columns of the scores dataset.</p>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span><span class=\"box-t\">Blueprint and houses</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Architects and civil engineers draw a <strong>blueprint</strong>, the plan of a house. If they build 100 houses from the same blueprint, all 100 have the same structure; still, each house can have its own name, colour, interior and exterior <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>. The blueprint is the <strong>class</strong>, and the 100 houses are the <strong>objects</strong>: uniform in structure, each with its own identity <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Class and object</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>class</strong> is a blueprint, or template: it lists the attributes (and, from 9.3, the behaviour) that every object of this kind will have. An <strong>object</strong> is one thing built from a class, with its own values. Books and the Python documentation also call an object an <strong>instance</strong> of its class.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"your-first-class\">Your first class</h3>\n<p>The lecture moves to Replit and types slowly <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>:\n  roll_no = <span class=\"hljs-literal\">None</span>\n  name = <span class=\"hljs-literal\">None</span>\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>class</code> is a <strong>keyword</strong>, a word with a fixed meaning in Python. As <code>def</code> starts a function, <code>class</code> starts a class <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>.</li>\n<li><code>Student</code> is the name of the class. Its first letter is a capital. This is not compulsory, but it is the standard practice that all programmers follow <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>.</li>\n<li>The indented lines are the <strong>body</strong> of the class. <code>roll_no = None</code> and <code>name = None</code> give both attributes the <strong>default value</strong> <code>None</code>, Python’s special value for “no value” (<a href=\"#/python/w05/return-a-value-you-can-use\">Week 5</a>), because the real values vary from student to student <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“As you can see, creating a class is very simple. It just defines what will be the blueprint” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>. From it we can create as many objects as we want, and each will have the two variables <code>roll_no</code> and <code>name</code>, with the default value <code>None</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two spaces</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Replit, used in this week’s videos, indents with 2 spaces, and the code on this page is copied as it was on the screen. Python accepts any indent, as long as all the lines of one body use the same one; 4 spaces is the usual choice.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"making-an-object-the-constructor\">Making an object: the constructor</h3>\n<p>The lecture picks the first student of the scores dataset. The IDs start from 0, so the object is called <code>s0</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s0 = Student()\n</code></pre>\n<p>“That is how we create object.” <code>s0</code> is now an <strong>object</strong> of the class <code>Student</code>.</p>\n<p><code>Student()</code> looks like a function call: a name followed by round brackets <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>. So is <code>Student</code> a class or a function? The lecture’s answer: it is a special function, whose name is exactly the name of the class <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>. We never wrote <code>def Student</code> anywhere, and still it works: whenever we create a class, Python adds this function by itself <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Constructor</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>constructor</strong> of a class is the special function, with the same name as the class, that builds a new object of the class: <code>Student()</code> gives a new <code>Student</code> object. The name comes from the blueprint picture: a blueprint is used to construct houses, and a class is used to construct objects <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-dot-operator\">The dot operator</h3>\n<p>Now the roll number and the name of this student are filled in <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s0.roll_no = <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>\ns0.name = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Bhuvanesh&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s0.roll_no, s0.name)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>0 Bhuvanesh\n</code></pre>\n<p>How it works <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>: the constructor built the object <code>s0</code>, and inside this object are two variables that <code>s0</code> owns <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>. <code>s0.<wbr>roll_no = 0</code> sets the roll number of <code>s0</code> only. The <code>.</code> between <code>s0</code> and <code>roll_no</code> is the <strong>dot operator</strong>. <code>print</code> with two values puts one space between them (<a href=\"#/python/w01/several-values-in-one-print\">Week 1</a>), so the output is <code>0 Bhuvanesh</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Dot operator</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>object.name</code> reaches something that belongs to the object: <code>object.<wbr>variable</code> for an attribute and <code>object.<wbr>function()</code> for a behaviour <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>. Read <code>s0.name</code> as “the name of <code>s0</code>”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"each-object-keeps-its-own-values\">Each object keeps its own values</h3>\n<p>The lecture builds three more students. This is the whole program at the end of the lecture, with its output <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>:\n  roll_no = <span class=\"hljs-literal\">None</span>\n  name = <span class=\"hljs-literal\">None</span>\n\ns0 = Student()\ns0.roll_no = <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>\ns0.name = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Bhuvanesh&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s0.roll_no, s0.name)\n\ns1 = Student()\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s1.roll_no, s1.name)\n\ns2 = Student()\ns2.roll_no = <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>\ns2.name = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Harish&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s2.roll_no, s2.name)\n\ns50 = Student()\ns50.roll_no = <span class=\"hljs-number\">50</span>\ns50.name = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Asmita&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s50.roll_no, s50.name)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>0 Bhuvanesh\nNone None\n2 Harish\n50 Asmita\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">How the program runs (the lecture’s walk-through, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Lines 1–3 define the class <code>Student</code>. Nothing is printed.</li>\n<li>Lines 5–7 build <code>s0</code> and give it 0 and <code>'Bhuvanesh'</code>; line 8 prints <code>0 Bhuvanesh</code>.</li>\n<li>Lines 10–11 build <code>s1</code> and set nothing for it, so it prints the default values, <code>None None</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>.</li>\n<li>Lines 13–16 build <code>s2</code>, give it 2 and <code>'Harish'</code>, and print <code>2 Harish</code>.</li>\n<li>Lines 18–21 build <code>s50</code>, give it 50 and <code>'Asmita'</code>, and print <code>50 Asmita</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Setting the values of <code>s0</code> had no effect on <code>s1</code>: “this is how we make sure each student or each object will have its own identity” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>. All four objects use the same two variable names, but each holds its own values: the values are changed for one object (<code>s0</code> or <code>s2</code>), not in the class <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> four lines: <code>0 Bhuvanesh</code>, <code>None None</code>, <code>2 Harish</code>, <code>50 Asmita</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>A new student</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>. Asmita joins the college. She is not in the scores dataset, and no roll number has been allotted to her yet, but she does have a name. So at first the lecture wrote only:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s50 = Student()\ns50.name = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Asmita&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s50.roll_no, s50.name)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This run printed <code>None Asmita</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>: “I do not have roll number. That is why I am saying the roll number is none.” When the college allots her a roll number, one line gives it to her, <code>s50.<wbr>roll_no = 50</code>, and the run prints <code>50 Asmita</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>.</p>\n<p>This is the flexibility of classes and objects: the same variable names, with different values for each object <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>. It is how the real world works too: every person has a name, but its value differs from person to person <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>.</p>\n<p>The lecture ends with a warning. This program works, but it is not the ideal way to use classes, and it has no behaviour (functions) yet. “Remember this code”: the next lecture changes it <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZPtRsQrZLcE&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-proper-way-init-and-self\">The proper way: <code>__init__</code> and <code>self</code></h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 9.3, Attributes and methods.</em> The lecture rewrites the program of 9.2, first the whole code in silence, then every part of it: “this is the most accurate way of using OOP concepts in Python” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>:\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, roll_no, name</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.roll_no = roll_no\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name = name\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.roll_no, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name)\n\ns0 = Student(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Bhuvanesh&#x27;</span>)\ns0.display()\n\ns1 = Student(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Harish&#x27;</span>)\ns1.display()\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>0 Bhuvanesh\n1 Harish\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is new <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inside the class there are now two functions. The first is special. Its name is <code>__init__</code>: two underscores, the word <code>init</code>, two underscores. It has three parameters: <code>self</code>, <code>roll_no</code> and <code>name</code>.</li>\n<li>Its body is <code>self.<wbr>roll_no = roll_no</code> and <code>self.<wbr>name = name</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>.</li>\n<li>The second function, <code>display</code>, is an ordinary one. It also has the parameter <code>self</code>.</li>\n<li>The objects are built with two values in the brackets: <code>Student(<wbr>0, 'Bhuvanesh')</code>, then <code>Student(<wbr>1, 'Harish')</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>. The first value is the roll number and the second is the name.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"init-runs-by-itself\"><code>__init__</code> runs by itself</h3>\n<p><code>__init__</code> looks like a function, but the program never calls it. Then what is its use <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>? Remember the constructor of 9.2. When values must be stored in the variables of the object as it is built, we use a <strong>constructor with parameters</strong>, and whenever such a constructor is called, Python internally runs <code>__init__</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>. So you never call it yourself, unlike <code>display</code>.</p>\n<p>So <code>s0 = Student(<wbr>0, 'Bhuvanesh')</code> makes Python run the three lines of <code>__init__</code>. <code>s1 = Student(<wbr>1, 'Harish')</code> runs them again, and a third object would run them a third time <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>. Every time an object is created with these two values, Python runs <code>__init__</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\"><code>__init__</code></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>__init__</code> (say “init”, short for <strong>initialise</strong>: to give the first values) is the special method that Python runs automatically each time an object is built. <code>Student(<wbr>0, 'Bhuvanesh')</code> builds a new object and runs <code>__init__</code> on it with <code>roll_no</code> = 0 and <code>name</code> = <code>'Bhuvanesh'</code>. Its name must be exactly two underscores, <code>init</code>, two underscores; it is a special function that exists inside every class <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"self-is-the-current-object\"><code>self</code> is the current object</h3>\n<p>Both objects store their values in variables with the same names, <code>roll_no</code> and <code>name</code>. So how does Python know whether they belong to <code>s0</code> or to <code>s1</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>? Every time a function of the class runs, Python knows which object started it, and it puts that object into <code>self</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>. Line by line:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Line 9, <code>s0 = Student(<wbr>0, 'Bhuvanesh')</code>: <code>self</code> is <code>s0</code>, so <code>__init__</code> does <code>s0.<wbr>roll_no = 0</code> and <code>s0.<wbr>name = 'Bhuvanesh'</code>.</li>\n<li>Line 10, <code>s0.display()</code>: <code>self</code> is <code>s0</code>, so it prints <code>0 Bhuvanesh</code>.</li>\n<li>Line 12, <code>s1 = Student(<wbr>1, 'Harish')</code>: <code>self</code> is now <code>s1</code>, so <code>__init__</code> does <code>s1.<wbr>roll_no = 1</code> and <code>s1.<wbr>name = 'Harish'</code>.</li>\n<li>Line 13, <code>s1.display()</code>: <code>self</code> is <code>s1</code>, so it prints <code>1 Harish</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>“To generalize this, self is a container which holds the current object”, and that is why <code>self</code> must be the <strong>first parameter of every function inside a class</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>. When <code>s0.display()</code> runs, <code>self</code> is <code>s0</code>, so the <code>print</code> line means <code>print(<wbr>s0.<wbr>roll_no, s0.<wbr>name)</code>; with <code>s1.display()</code> it means <code>print(<wbr>s1.<wbr>roll_no, s1.<wbr>name)</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\"><code>self</code></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>self</code> is the first parameter of every function in a class. While the function runs, <code>self</code> holds the <strong>current object</strong>: the object being built (in <code>__init__</code>) or the object before the dot (in <code>s0.display()</code>). <code>self.name</code> then means “the name of this object”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\"><code>s0.display()</code> is short for <code>Student.<wbr>display(s0)</code></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Our own check: <code>Student.<wbr>display(s0)</code> prints the same <code>0 Bhuvanesh</code>. Python takes the object before the dot and passes it as the first argument, <code>self</code>. That is why <code>def display(<wbr>self):</code> has one parameter while the call <code>s0.display()</code> has empty brackets.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Is <code>self</code> a keyword?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture calls <code>self</code> “a new keyword” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>. Strictly, it is the name that every Python programmer gives the first parameter, not a keyword like <code>class</code> or <code>def</code>: our own check, <code>def display(me):</code> with <code>print(<wbr>me.<wbr>roll_no, me.<wbr>name)</code> works the same. Still, always write <code>self</code>, as everyone does.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"self-roll-no-roll-no-two-different-variables\"><code>self.<wbr>roll_no = roll_no</code>: two different variables</h3>\n<p>“Why we are saying roll number is equal to roll number ... This does not make any sense” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>. The two sides are different variables:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The right side</strong>, <code>roll_no</code>, is a <strong>parameter</strong> of <code>__init__</code>. The value 0 of the call goes into it, as in any function call <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>. It is a normal function variable, so it cannot be used outside the function <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a> (this is the <strong>scope</strong> of <a href=\"#/python/w06/scope-local-and-global-variables\">Week 6</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>The left side</strong>, <code>self.roll_no</code>, is an <strong>object variable</strong>: an attribute of <code>s0</code> or <code>s1</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>. Every object has its own copy of every such variable <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>, and it lasts as long as the object.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So the line copies the parameter into the object. If the same names confuse you, give the parameters other names <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>:\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, a, b</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.roll_no = a\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name = b\n</code></pre>\n<p>With the rest of the program unchanged, this version printed the same <code>0 Bhuvanesh</code> and <code>1 Harish</code>, and so did the original one when run again <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>. Using the same name on both sides is the usual style.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A parameter is not an attribute</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Our own check: in <code>display</code>, writing <code>print(<wbr>roll_no, name)</code> instead of <code>print(<wbr>self.<wbr>roll_no, self.<wbr>name)</code> stops with <code>NameError: name 'roll_no' is not defined</code>. The parameters of <code>__init__</code> are gone once <code>__init__</code> ends; only what was stored through <code>self</code> stays in the object.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"class-attributes-and-object-attributes\">Class attributes and object attributes</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 9.3, continued.</em> Both programs give the same output. So what is the difference between the code of 9.2 and this one <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>? In 9.2 the variables were written <strong>directly in the class</strong>; now they are set <strong>inside <code>__init__</code></strong>, through <code>self</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A variable set inside <code>__init__</code> is owned by one object, <code>s0</code> or <code>s1</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>.</li>\n<li>A variable written directly in the class is owned by the <strong>class</strong>, not by its objects. The objects do not get copies of it: there is only one copy, shared by every object of the class <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture’s example is a class variable <code>count</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>:\n  count = <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, roll_no, name</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.roll_no = roll_no\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name = name\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.roll_no, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name)\n\ns0 = Student(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Bhuvanesh&#x27;</span>)\nStudent.count += <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\ns0.display()\n\ns1 = Student(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Harish&#x27;</span>)\nStudent.count += <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\ns1.display()\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(Student.count)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>0 Bhuvanesh\n1 Harish\n2\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>count</code> belongs to <code>Student</code>, so the lecture writes it with the class name, <code>Student.<wbr>count</code>, and not <code>s0.count</code> or <code>s1.count</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>. With only the last line, <code>print(<wbr>Student.<wbr>count)</code>, added, the run printed <code>0</code>, the starting value <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>. Then a line <code>Student.<wbr>count += 1</code> went after each object (<code>+=</code> adds to a variable, <a href=\"#/python/w02/shorthand-operators\">Week 2</a>), and the run printed <code>2</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Trace of count</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>After line</th>\n<th><code>Student.<wbr>count</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2, <code>count = 0</code></td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11, after <code>s0</code> is built</td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15, after <code>s1</code> is built</td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>There is one <code>count</code> for the whole class, so both lines add to the same variable.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the last line prints <code>2</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Class attribute and object attribute</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>class attribute</strong> is a variable written directly in the body of the class, like <code>count = 0</code>. It has one copy, owned by the class and shared by all its objects. Use it with the class name: <code>Student.<wbr>count</code>.</li>\n<li>An <strong>object attribute</strong> (or instance attribute) is set through <code>self</code>, usually in <code>__init__</code>, like <code>self.<wbr>roll_no = roll_no</code>. Every object has its own copy, with its own value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the lecture’s words, <code>count</code> is a class attribute, while <code>roll_no</code> and <code>name</code> are object attributes <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Change a class attribute through the class name</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Our own check. Reading <code>s0.count</code> also gives the class’s value, because Python looks for a name in the object first and then in its class. But <code>s0.<wbr>count += 1</code> does not change the shared count: it makes a new <code>count</code> inside <code>s0</code> only. Afterwards <code>s0.count</code> is 1, while <code>Student.<wbr>count</code> and <code>s1.count</code> are still 0. To change a class attribute, write <code>Student.<wbr>count</code>, as the lecture does.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">What really happened in the program of 9.2</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In 9.2, <code>roll_no = None</code> and <code>name = None</code> were written directly in the class, so they were <strong>class attributes</strong>. <code>s1.roll_no</code> printed <code>None</code> because <code>s1</code> had no <code>roll_no</code> of its own and Python found the class’s. <code>s0.<wbr>roll_no = 0</code> made a new <code>roll_no</code> inside <code>s0</code> only. That is why the program worked, and also why 9.3 calls it not ideal: each student’s own attributes belong in <code>__init__</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"behaviour-methods\">Behaviour: methods</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 9.3, continued.</em> The attributes are done, but the behaviour part of the object is still missing <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>. <code>__init__</code> and <code>display</code> are functions, but they add no behaviour of their own: <code>__init__</code> only stores the values, and <code>display</code> only prints them <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pass-or-fail-the-result-method\">Pass or fail: the result method</h3>\n<p>First, one more attribute from the scores dataset, the <strong>total</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>. <code>__init__</code> gets a parameter <code>total</code> and stores <code>self.total</code>, <code>display</code> prints it too, the two students get totals 100 and 150, and <code>count</code> is removed. That run printed <code>0 Bhuvanesh 100</code> and <code>1 Harish 150</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>.</p>\n<p>The behaviour of a student is the result: did they pass or fail <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>? The final program <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>:\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, roll_no, name, total</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.roll_no = roll_no\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name = name\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.total = total\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.roll_no, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.total)\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">result</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.total &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">120</span>:\n      <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Pass&#x27;</span>)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">else</span>:\n      <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Fail&#x27;</span>)\n\ns0 = Student(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Bhuvanesh&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>)\ns0.display()\ns0.result()\n\ns1 = Student(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Harish&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">150</span>)\ns1.display()\ns1.result()\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>0 Bhuvanesh 100\nFail\n1 Harish 150\nPass\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>result</code> is inside the class, so its first parameter is <code>self</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>. The pass mark is the lecture’s choice: a total greater than 120.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Trace of the program</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>s0 = Student(<wbr>0, 'Bhuvanesh', 100)</code>: <code>__init__</code> runs with <code>self</code> = <code>s0</code>, so <code>s0.total</code> is 100.</li>\n<li><code>s0.display()</code> prints <code>0 Bhuvanesh 100</code>.</li>\n<li><code>s0.result()</code>: <code>self</code> is <code>s0</code>, so the test is <code>100 &gt; 120</code>, which is <code>False</code>. The <code>else</code> part prints <code>Fail</code>.</li>\n<li><code>s1 = Student(<wbr>1, 'Harish', 150)</code> stores 150 in <code>s1.total</code>, and <code>s1.display()</code> prints <code>1 Harish 150</code>.</li>\n<li><code>s1.result()</code>: the test is <code>150 &gt; 120</code>, which is <code>True</code>, so it prints <code>Pass</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>0 Bhuvanesh 100</code>, <code>Fail</code>, <code>1 Harish 150</code>, <code>Pass</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>On each line the first values are the student’s attributes, and <code>Fail</code> or <code>Pass</code> is the <strong>behaviour</strong> of that particular student, worked out from their own total <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>. “This is how we write object-oriented programs using classes and objects, including its behavior” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"function-or-method\">Function or method?</h3>\n<p>The lecturer has used two words for the functions in a class, “function” and “method”. The difference <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>:</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Method</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>method</strong> is a function that is defined inside a class, that is, a function that belongs to a class. <code>__init__</code>, <code>display</code> and <code>result</code> are the methods of the class <code>Student</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>This is also why the string functions of <a href=\"#/python/w02/string-methods\">Week 2</a>, such as <code>upper()</code>, are called <strong>string methods</strong>: there is a class for strings, and those functions are inside it <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>. The difference is only a technical term, but it is a good habit to call each kind by its proper name <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>. (<a href=\"#/python/w06/four-kinds-of-functions\">Week 6</a> promised that “more kinds of methods come later”: here they are.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-homework-int-str-list-and-dict\">The lecture’s homework: int, str, list and dict</h3>\n<p>The lecture closes with a small exercise <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>. It runs this code:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">i = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>()\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(i))\n\ns = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>()\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(s))\n\nl = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">list</span>()\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(l))\n\nd = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">dict</span>()\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(d))\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>&lt;class 'int'&gt;\n&lt;class 'str'&gt;\n&lt;class 'list'&gt;\n&lt;class 'dict'&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>type()</code> gives the data type of a value (<a href=\"#/python/w01/data-types\">Week 1</a>). The questions: what are <code>int</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>list</code> and <code>dict</code> here, and why does the output always say <code>class</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n_jPQlWhGhM&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>? Relate them to this lecture yourself first; then compare with the last question of Check yourself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"inheritance\">Inheritance</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 9.4, Inheritance and method overriding.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"parents-and-children\">Parents and children</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A child gets some qualities of the parents by birth <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>. Your father likes to paint and is very good at it, and you are a good painter too, without any training. Your mother has brown hair, and so do you <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>. OOP translates real life into code, so it needs a way to express this relation between you and your parents. That mechanism is <strong>inheritance</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>.</p>\n<p>What you inherit does not limit you <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>. You can also learn swimming: that <strong>adds</strong> a quality to the ones you have. Or you can colour your hair red: that <strong>replaces</strong> an inherited property. Python has a way for both, called <strong>method overriding</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"two-classes-that-repeat-themselves\">Two classes that repeat themselves</h3>\n<p>The starting program <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>():\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age, marks</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name = name\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.age = age\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.marks = marks\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.age, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.marks)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Employee</span>():\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age, salary</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name = name\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.age = age\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.salary = salary\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.age, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.salary)\n\ns = Student(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Rida&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">250</span>)\ns.display()\n\ne = Employee(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Harsh&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">50000</span>)\ne.display()\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Rida 20 250\nHarsh 30 50000\n</code></pre>\n<p>Nothing new: two classes, an object of each, and each object calls the <code>display</code> of its own class <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>. (A class may be written with empty brackets, <code>class Student():</code>, or without them, <code>class Student:</code>; both are correct <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>.)</p>\n<p>But there is a lot of <strong>duplication</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>: <code>name</code> and <code>age</code> appear in both classes, and only <code>marks</code> and <code>salary</code> differ. “We are programmers. We do not repeat the same thing twice” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>. Two classes may still be fine, but picture separate classes for school, high school, college, postgraduate and PhD students <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>, and for employees in the medical, manufacturing and educational fields: there can be hundreds of such classes <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>, all repeating not just name and age but gender, address, mobile number, email address and more <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-parent-class-person\">A parent class: Person</h3>\n<p>You and your sibling share many qualities because both of you inherited them from a common parent <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>. So treat <code>Student</code> and <code>Employee</code> as siblings. Their common attributes, <code>name</code> and <code>age</code>, go into a <strong>parent class</strong> called <code>Person</code>, while <code>marks</code> and <code>salary</code> stay in their own classes <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>, because not every person has marks or a salary, but every person has a name and an age <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The “is a” relationship</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Every student <strong>is a</strong> person, and every employee <strong>is a</strong> person <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>. This relation between a child class and its parent class is called an <strong>is a</strong> relationship.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The common <code>__init__</code> and <code>display</code> move into <code>Person</code>, and the child classes keep only what is theirs <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>. To tell Python which class is the parent, write its name in the brackets of the child: <code>class Student(<wbr>Person):</code> and <code>class Employee(<wbr>Person):</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>. The program at this point <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Person</span>:\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name = name\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.age = age\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.age)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Person</span>):\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age, marks</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.marks = marks\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.marks)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Employee</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Person</span>):\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age, salary</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.salary = salary\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.salary)\n\ns = Student(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Rida&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">250</span>)\ns.display()\n\ne = Employee(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Harsh&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">50000</span>)\ne.display()\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Inheritance, parent class and child class</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Inheritance</strong> lets a class take over the attributes and methods of another class. In <code>class Student(<wbr>Person):</code>, “student inherits the properties of person”: <code>Person</code> is the <strong>parent class</strong>, also called the <strong>superclass</strong>, and <code>Student</code> is the <strong>child class</strong>, also called the <strong>subclass</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The run printed only this <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>250\n50000\n</code></pre>\n<p>Line 11 (<code>self.<wbr>marks = marks</code>) and line 18 (<code>self.<wbr>salary = salary</code>) ran, and the children’s <code>display</code> printed the marks and the salary. But no line of <code>Person</code> ran: name and age were neither stored nor printed <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>. The reason: <code>Student</code> has an <code>__init__</code> and a <code>display</code> of its own, and Python runs the child’s own method whenever there is one. We must tell Python to run the parent’s code as well: whenever you store the marks, also store the name and age; whenever you print the marks, also print the name and age <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"super-run-the-parent-s-code\"><code>super()</code>: run the parent’s code</h3>\n<p>The special function <code>super()</code> does this <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Person</span>):\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age, marks</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().__init__(name, age)\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.marks = marks\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>super().<wbr>__init__(<wbr>name, age)</code> means “run the <code>__init__</code> of my parent class with these two values”. The order of events for <code>s = Student(<wbr>'Rida', 20, 250)</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>, with the line numbers of the whole program below (<code>Person</code> takes lines 1–7, and <code>Student</code> starts on line 9):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Python builds the object and runs the <code>__init__</code> of <code>Student</code> with <code>name</code> = <code>'Rida'</code>, <code>age</code> = 20 and <code>marks</code> = 250.</li>\n<li>Line 11, <code>super().<wbr>__init__(<wbr>name, age)</code>, sends Python to the parent’s <code>__init__</code> on line 2. There <code>self.<wbr>name = name</code> and <code>self.<wbr>age = age</code> store <code>'Rida'</code> and 20 in the object <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>.</li>\n<li>Python comes back to line 12 and runs <code>self.<wbr>marks = marks</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><code>Employee</code> gets the same line. Notice that <code>super().<wbr>__init__(<wbr>name, age)</code> passes only <code>name</code> and <code>age</code>: Python passes the object itself as <code>self</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The two underscores after init</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer first typed <code>super().<wbr>__init(<wbr>name, age)</code>, without the two underscores at the end (“I have missed two underscores here”), and corrected it before running <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>. Our own check of that line: it stops with <code>AttributeError: 'super' object has no attribute '_Student__init'</code>. (The odd name <code>_Student__init</code> comes from the two-underscore rule of private members, at the end of this lecture.) And <code>super().<wbr>init(<wbr>name, age)</code> stops with <code>AttributeError: 'super' object has no attribute 'init'</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The next run still printed only <code>250</code> and <code>50000</code>: the values are stored now, but the parent’s <code>display</code> never runs <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>. So the <code>display</code> of each child calls the parent’s too. In <code>Student</code> (and the same in <code>Employee</code>, with <code>print(<wbr>self.<wbr>salary)</code>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().display()\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.marks)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The whole program <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Person</span>:\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name = name\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.age = age\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.age)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Person</span>):\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age, marks</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().__init__(name, age)\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.marks = marks\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().display()\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.marks)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Employee</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Person</span>):\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age, salary</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().__init__(name, age)\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.salary = salary\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().display()\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.salary)\n\ns = Student(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Rida&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">250</span>)\ns.display()\n\ne = Employee(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Harsh&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">50000</span>)\ne.display()\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Rida 20\n250\nHarsh 30\n50000\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Trace of <code>s.display()</code> (the lecture’s walk-through at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>s</code> is a <code>Student</code>, so Python runs the <code>display</code> of <code>Student</code> (line 14), with <code>self</code> = <code>s</code>.</li>\n<li>Line 15, <code>super().<wbr>display()</code>: “super means in super class, in parent class”. Python runs the <code>display</code> of <code>Person</code>, whose line 7 prints <code>self.<wbr>name, self.<wbr>age</code>: <code>Rida 20</code>.</li>\n<li>Python comes back to line 16, which prints <code>self.marks</code>: <code>250</code>.</li>\n<li><code>e.display()</code> goes the same way through <code>Employee</code>: <code>Harsh 30</code>, then <code>50000</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>Rida 20</code>, <code>250</code>, <code>Harsh 30</code>, <code>50000</code>: name and age from the parent’s method, then marks or salary from the child’s <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\"><code>super()</code></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Inside a method of a child class, <code>super()</code> reaches the methods of the parent class: <code>super().<wbr>__init__(...)</code> runs the parent’s <code>__init__</code>, and <code>super().<wbr>display()</code> runs the parent’s <code>display</code>, both on the same object, <code>self</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"method-overriding\">Method overriding</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 9.4, continued.</em> Back to the two scenarios of the start <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>. Printing the marks along with the inherited name and age was the first one: learning swimming along with painting, an <strong>addition</strong>. The second is the red hair: <strong>replacing</strong> an inherited property <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>. For this, the lecture rewrites the whole <code>display</code> of <code>Employee</code>, with the name and age in it, and removes <code>super().<wbr>display()</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Employee</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Person</span>):\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age, salary</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().__init__(name, age)\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.salary = salary\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.age, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.salary)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now <code>Employee</code> does not refer to the <code>display</code> of <code>Person</code> at all. It inherited that method, but replaces it with its own: the brown hair replaced by red <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Method overriding</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Method overriding</strong>: a child class defines a method with the same name as a method of its parent class. For objects of the child class, the child’s method is used instead of the inherited one <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The run <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Rida 20\n250\nHarsh 30 50000\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture says that “still we should get same output”. The values are the same, but the employee’s three values now come from one <code>print</code>, so they stand on one line.</p>\n<p>So the two child classes now show both scenarios:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <code>display</code> of <code>Student</code> runs <code>super().<wbr>display()</code> and then its own <code>print</code>: it <strong>adds</strong> to the parent’s method (swimming along with painting).</li>\n<li>The <code>display</code> of <code>Employee</code> has only its own <code>print</code>: it <strong>replaces</strong> the parent’s method (red hair instead of brown).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What inheritance gives you</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>. A child inherits properties from its parent, and when siblings have common properties, you bring them together in a common parent class that both children use. This lets you <strong>reuse</strong> the same code, and it <strong>modularises</strong> the code: it divides the code into small blocks instead of putting everything in a single class or a single function <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Method overloading</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>At <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a> the lecturer lists “method overloading” among the ideas covered so far, but the lectures never show it. In some other languages, overloading means several methods with one name and different parameters. Python has no overloading of this kind: our own check, a second <code>def</code> with the same name in a class simply replaces the first one.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"one-class-per-file-import\">One class per file: import</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 9.4, continued.</em> A doubt from Week 1: why does Replit run only <code>main.py</code>, even when the project has other files <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>? Now each class gets a file of its own <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>: <code>Person</code> goes into <code>Person.py</code>, <code>Student</code> into <code>Student.py</code> and <code>Employee</code> into <code>Employee.py</code>. (The narration says “person dot py”; the file names on the screen begin with a capital letter.) Left in <code>main.py</code> are only the objects and the calls <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s = Student(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Rida&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">250</span>)\ns.display()\n\ne = Employee(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Harsh&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">50000</span>)\ne.display()\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;main.py&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\n    s = Student('Rida', 20, 250)\nNameError: name 'Student' is not defined\n</code></pre>\n<p>Replit runs only <code>main.py</code>, and in that file Python does not know what <code>Student</code> is <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>. We have defined <code>Student</code> in another file; now we must tell Python where it is <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>. Two lines go at the top of <code>main.py</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> Student <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> Student\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> Employee <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> Employee\n</code></pre>\n<p>“There is a file called student and inside that file, I have a class called student. So, please go ahead and import that for me.” The run still failed <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;main.py&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\n    from Student import Student\n  File &quot;/home/runner/Python-Lectures/Student.py&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\n    class Student(Person):\nNameError: name 'Person' is not defined\n</code></pre>\n<p>Read a traceback from the bottom up. The error is in <code>Student.py</code>, line 1, <code>class Student(<wbr>Person):</code>. <code>main.py</code> imported both classes, but in the file <code>Student.py</code> Python does not know <code>Person</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>. So <code>Student.py</code> gets an import line at the top, and <code>Employee.py</code> the same:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> Person <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> Person\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Person</span>):\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age, marks</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().__init__(name, age)\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.marks = marks\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().display()\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.marks)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now <code>main.py</code> runs and prints the same as before <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Rida 20\n250\nHarsh 30 50000\n</code></pre>\n<p>The four files in the end:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Person.py</code> holds the class <code>Person</code> and imports nothing.</li>\n<li><code>Student.py</code> holds the class <code>Student(<wbr>Person)</code>, with <code>from Person import Person</code> at the top.</li>\n<li><code>Employee.py</code> holds the class <code>Employee(<wbr>Person)</code>, with <code>from Person import Person</code> at the top.</li>\n<li><code>main.py</code> holds the objects and the calls, with <code>from Student import Student</code> and <code>from Employee import Employee</code> at the top.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\"><code>from file import name</code></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>from Student import Student</code> opens the file <code>Student.py</code> (the first <code>Student</code>, written without <code>.py</code>) and takes from it the class <code>Student</code> (the second). After that, the name is known in the file that imported it. Every file needs import lines for the names it uses from other files <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>. It is the <code>from ... import ...</code> of the libraries in <a href=\"#/python/w02/five-ways-to-import\">Week 2</a>, with your own file in place of a library.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>You can now create as many <code>.py</code> files as you want, as long as you import each class in this way before you create its objects and call its methods <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">File names must match exactly</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Our own check on a Mac: with the file <code>Person.py</code>, the line <code>from person import Person</code> stops with <code>ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'person'</code>. Write the file name in the import exactly as it is, capitals included.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"private-members\">Private members</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 9.4, continued.</em> Inheritance raises a question of security <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>. Everything in <code>Person</code> is available in <code>Student</code> and <code>Employee</code>, because they inherit it <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>. What if <code>Person</code> has some secret information that it wants to keep to itself, even from its child classes <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>? “You might inherit a lot of things from your parents but they may not tell you all their secrets” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>.</p>\n<p>Python’s way is two underscores before the variable name <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>. Suppose <code>Person</code> does not want to reveal its age. The lecture changes line 4 of <code>Person.py</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Person</span>:\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name = name\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.__age = age\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.age)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;main.py&quot;, line 5, in &lt;module&gt;\n    s.display()\n  File &quot;/home/runner/Python-Lectures/Student.py&quot;, line 9, in display\n    super().display()\n  File &quot;/home/runner/Python-Lectures/Person.py&quot;, line 7, in display\n    print(self.name, self.age)\nAttributeError: 'Student' object has no attribute 'age'\n</code></pre>\n<p>“Student object has no attribute age” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>. With the underscores removed, the age is available to everyone again and the program prints as before <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Private and public members</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An attribute (or method) whose name starts with two underscores, like <code>__age</code>, is a <strong>private member</strong> of its class: code outside the class, child classes included, cannot reach it by that name. Without the underscores it is a <strong>public member</strong>, accessible to everyone <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-the-two-underscores-really-do\">What the two underscores really do</h3>\n<p>Read the traceback above from the bottom. The error is raised in <code>Person.py</code>, line 7, inside the <code>display</code> of <code>Person</code> itself: after the change, the age is stored under the name <code>__age</code>, so <code>self.age</code> asks for a name that no longer exists, even inside <code>Person</code>. The lecture’s run shows that much. To see the privacy itself, here is our own example, where the <code>display</code> of <code>Person</code> uses <code>self.__age</code>, and a method of <code>Student</code> tries the same:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Person</span>:\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name = name\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.__age = age\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">display</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.name, <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.__age)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Person</span>):\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, name, age, marks</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().__init__(name, age)\n    <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.marks = marks\n\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">show_age</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self</span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.__age)\n\ns = Student(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Rida&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">250</span>)\ns.display()\ns.show_age()\n</code></pre>\n<ol>\n<li><code>s.display()</code> prints <code>Rida 20</code>: a method of <code>Person</code> can use <code>__age</code>.</li>\n<li><code>s.show_age()</code> stops with <code>AttributeError: 'Student' object has no attribute '_Student__age'</code>: the child class cannot.</li>\n<li>Outside the classes, <code>print(<wbr>s.<wbr>__age)</code> stops with <code>AttributeError: 'Student' object has no attribute '__age'</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>How: inside a class, Python quietly renames a name that starts with two underscores (and does not end with two). In <code>Person</code>, <code>self.__age</code> becomes <code>self.<wbr>_Person__age</code>; in <code>Student</code>, <code>self.__age</code> becomes <code>self.<wbr>_Student__age</code>, which does not exist. This renaming is called <strong>name mangling</strong>. It guards against accidents rather than locking the value away: <code>s.<wbr>_Person__age</code> still gives 20.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two underscores on both sides</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>__init__</code> has two underscores on both sides, so it is not private: names like that are Python’s special methods. A private name has two underscores at the start only, as in <code>__age</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"types-of-inheritance\">Types of inheritance</h2>\n<p><em>Lecture 9.4, continued.</em> At the start, you had two superclasses, your father and your mother; in the program, one superclass <code>Person</code> had two subclasses <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>. Depending on the number of superclasses and subclasses and how they are related, there are five types of inheritance <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>. The slides draw each one with arrows going from parent to child.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Classes, parent → child</th>\n<th>In the lecture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple</td>\n<td>Parent → Child</td>\n<td>one parent class, one child class <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hierarchical</td>\n<td>Parent → Child 1, Child 2, Child 3</td>\n<td><code>Person</code> → <code>Student</code>, <code>Employee</code> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiple</td>\n<td>Parent 1, Parent 2 → Child</td>\n<td>father and mother → you <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multilevel</td>\n<td>Parent → Intermediate → Child</td>\n<td>grandparent, parent and child <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hybrid</td>\n<td>any mix of the four</td>\n<td>no standard diagram <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simple inheritance</strong>: only one parent class and one child class.</li>\n<li><strong>Hierarchical inheritance</strong>: a single parent class and several child classes, as in the <code>Person</code> program.</li>\n<li><strong>Multiple inheritance</strong>: more than one parent class and a single child class, like painting from your father and brown hair from your mother.</li>\n<li><strong>Multilevel inheritance</strong>: the <strong>intermediate class</strong> is a child of the parent class and, at the same time, the parent of the child class: “more like a grandparent, parent and child kind of a situation” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hybrid inheritance</strong>: any combination of the other four, which is why it has no standard diagram <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture leaves the programs for these types to you <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>. As a start, this is how the class lines look (our own sketch; <code>pass</code>, the placeholder of <a href=\"#/python/w04/pass-a-placeholder\">Week 4</a>, stands in for the bodies):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Child</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Parent</span>):            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># simple</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">pass</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Child</span>(Parent1, Parent2):  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># multiple: parents separated by commas</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">pass</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Intermediate</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Parent</span>):     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># multilevel: a chain</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">pass</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Child</span>(<span class=\"hljs-title class_ inherited__\">Intermediate</span>):\n  <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">pass</span>\n</code></pre>\n<p>A hierarchical family is several classes with the same parent, like <code>Student(<wbr>Person)</code> and <code>Employee(<wbr>Person)</code>.</p>\n<p>In multilevel inheritance, the child also gets everything that the intermediate class inherited from the parent. In words: a child <strong>is an</strong> intermediate, an intermediate <strong>is a</strong> parent, so a child <strong>is a</strong> parent too. In the language of relations (Mathematics 1, Week 1), “is a” is <strong>transitive</strong>: from (Child, Intermediate) and (Intermediate, Parent) follows (Child, Parent).</p>\n<a class=\"labcard\" href=\"#/labs/sets/relations?X=Child,Intermediate,Parent&amp;by=pairs&amp;R=(Child,Intermediate),(Intermediate,Parent),(Child,Parent)&amp;look=transitive\"><span class=\"labcard-k\">Try it in the Sets and functions lab</span><span class=\"labcard-t\">Multilevel inheritance: the “is a” relation is transitive</span><span class=\"labcard-go\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></a>\n<h2 id=\"oop-mistakes-and-their-messages\">OOP mistakes and their messages</h2>\n<p>The two messages with a lecture time were on Replit’s screen in the videos. The others are our own checks with Python 3.14; Python 3.9 and older print <code>__init__()</code> and <code>display()</code> where newer versions print <code>Student.<wbr>__init__()</code> and <code>Student.<wbr>display()</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Values for a class without <code>__init__</code></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>With the class of 9.2, which has no <code>__init__</code>, <code>Student(<wbr>0, 'Bhuvanesh')</code> stops with <code>TypeError: Student() takes no arguments</code>. A misspelt <code>__init__</code>, such as <code>_init_</code> or <code>__int__</code>, gives the same message: Python finds no constructor with parameters.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A method without <code>self</code></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>def display():</code> with no parameter, then <code>s0.display()</code>, stops with <code>TypeError: Student.<wbr>display() takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given</code>. The one argument “given” is the object itself, which Python always passes as <code>self</code>. Every method needs <code>self</code> first.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The wrong number of values</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>With the <code>__init__(<wbr>self, roll_no, name)</code> of 9.3, <code>Student(0)</code> stops with <code>TypeError: Student.<wbr>__init__() missing 1 required positional argument: 'name'</code>, and <code>Student(<wbr>0, 'Bhuvanesh', 5)</code> with <code>TypeError: Student.<wbr>__init__() takes 3 positional arguments but 4 were given</code>. Give one value for each parameter after <code>self</code>; the “3” counts <code>self</code> too.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A parameter or a name without <code>self.</code></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>print(<wbr>roll_no)</code> inside <code>display</code> stops with <code>NameError: name 'roll_no' is not defined</code>. Inside a method, the object’s values are <code>self.roll_no</code> and <code>self.name</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">An attribute that was never set</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>s0.total</code> with the class of 9.2 stops with <code>AttributeError: 'Student' object has no attribute 'total'</code>. Often it is a typo; newer Python adds a hint, as in <code>AttributeError: 'Student' object has no attribute 'nam'. Did you mean: 'name'?</code></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">super() mistakes</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><code>super().<wbr>init(<wbr>name, age)</code>: <code>AttributeError: 'super' object has no attribute 'init'</code>. The name is <code>__init__</code>, with two underscores on both sides.</li>\n<li><code>super().<wbr>__init__(<wbr>self, name, age)</code>: <code>TypeError: Person.<wbr>__init__() takes 3 positional arguments but 4 were given</code>. Do not pass <code>self</code> to it; Python passes the object for you.</li>\n<li>No <code>super().<wbr>__init__(...)</code> at all: the child’s <code>__init__</code> replaces the parent’s, so the name and age are never stored, and <code>super().<wbr>display()</code> stops with <code>AttributeError: 'Student' object has no attribute 'name'</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Imports between files</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A class used in <code>main.py</code> without an import: <code>NameError: name 'Student' is not defined</code> (9.4, at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</li>\n<li>A child class in a file that does not import its parent: <code>NameError: name 'Person' is not defined</code>, raised in <code>Student.py</code> (9.4, at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RrFvcDjJptY&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n<li>An import with the wrong capitals: <code>ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'person'</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Printing an object</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>print(s0)</code> does not show the values. It prints something like <code>&lt;__main__.<wbr>Student object at 0x101d5dfd0&gt;</code>: the class of the object and where it is kept in memory, a number that changes from run to run. Call <code>s0.display()</code> instead. A method needs its brackets: <code>s0.display</code> alone does not run it. And <code>print(<wbr>s0.<wbr>display())</code> prints the values and then <code>None</code>, because <code>display</code> prints but returns nothing (<a href=\"#/python/w05/return-a-value-you-can-use\">Week 5</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>OOP</strong>: an object holds its own attributes (variables) and its own behaviour (methods). A <strong>class</strong> is the blueprint, and <strong>objects</strong> are built from it.</li>\n<li><strong>Class and object</strong>: <code>class Student:</code> (capital first letter, by convention); <code>s0 = Student(...)</code> calls the <strong>constructor</strong>; the <strong>dot operator</strong> reaches an object’s parts: <code>s0.name</code>, <code>s0.display()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>__init__(<wbr>self, ...)</code></strong> runs by itself each time an object is built. <strong><code>self</code></strong> is the current object. <code>self.<wbr>roll_no = roll_no</code> copies a parameter into the object.</li>\n<li><strong>Class attribute</strong>: written in the class body, one shared copy, used as <code>Student.<wbr>count</code>. <strong>Object attribute</strong>: set through <code>self</code>, one copy per object.</li>\n<li><strong>Method</strong>: a function inside a class, with <code>self</code> as its first parameter; <code>s0.result()</code> means <code>Student.<wbr>result(s0)</code>.</li>\n<li><code>int</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>list</code> and <code>dict</code> are classes, so <code>type()</code> prints <code>&lt;class 'int'&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inheritance</strong>: <code>class Student(<wbr>Person):</code>; parent class = superclass, child class = subclass; an “is a” relationship. <code>super().<wbr>__init__(<wbr>name, age)</code> and <code>super().<wbr>display()</code> run the parent’s code on the same object.</li>\n<li><strong>Method overriding</strong>: a child’s method with the same name replaces the parent’s; calling <code>super()</code> inside it adds to the parent’s instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Files</strong>: one class per <code>.py</code> file; <code>from Person import Person</code> (file name, then class name) in every file that uses the class.</li>\n<li><strong>Private member</strong>: <code>self.__age</code> cannot be reached by that name outside its class, not even in a child class. Without the underscores, it is public.</li>\n<li><strong>Types of inheritance</strong>: simple, hierarchical, multiple, multilevel, hybrid.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<p>With the <code>Student</code> class of 9.3 (its <code>__init__</code> and <code>display</code>), here is a small program of our own:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">p = Student(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Bhuvanesh&#x27;</span>)\nq = p\nq.name = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Harish&#x27;</span>\np.display()\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What does this program print, and why?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>p = Student(<wbr>0, 'Bhuvanesh')</code> builds one object, and <code>__init__</code> stores 0 and <code>'Bhuvanesh'</code> in it.</li>\n<li><code>q = p</code> builds nothing new: there is no <code>Student(...)</code> call, so <code>__init__</code> does not run. <code>q</code> is a second name for the same object, like the two names for one list in <a href=\"#/python/w06/two-names-for-one-list\">Week 6</a>.</li>\n<li><code>q.<wbr>name = 'Harish'</code> changes the name of that one object.</li>\n<li><code>p.display()</code> prints the values of that same object.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>0 Harish</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<p>Another program of our own, about the class attribute <code>count</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">Student</span>:\n  count = <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>\n\ns0 = Student()\ns1 = Student()\ns0.count += <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(Student.count, s0.count, s1.count)\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What does this program with count print, and why?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>count = 0</code> is a class attribute: one copy, owned by <code>Student</code>.</li>\n<li><code>s0.<wbr>count += 1</code> means <code>s0.<wbr>count = s0.<wbr>count + 1</code>. To read <code>s0.count</code>, Python looks in <code>s0</code>, finds no <code>count</code>, and takes the class’s 0. Then <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span> is stored as a <strong>new</strong> <code>count</code> inside <code>s0</code>.</li>\n<li><code>Student.<wbr>count</code> is still 0. <code>s1</code> has no <code>count</code> of its own, so <code>s1.count</code> is the class’s 0.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>0 1 0</code>. To count for the whole class, write <code>Student.<wbr>count += 1</code>, as the lecture does.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">With the final class of 9.3, what does <code>Student(<wbr>2, 'Asmita', 120).<wbr>result()</code> print?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>__init__</code> stores <code>self.<wbr>total = 120</code>.</li>\n<li><code>result</code> tests <code>self.<wbr>total &gt; 120</code>, that is <code>120 &gt; 120</code>. A number is not greater than itself, so the test is <code>False</code>.</li>\n<li>The <code>else</code> part runs.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>Fail</code>. With a total of 121 it prints <code>Pass</code>. The lecture’s rule is “greater than 120”, so exactly 120 fails.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">In the first version of 9.4 with <code>Person</code> (before <code>super()</code> was added), the program builds <code>s = Student(<wbr>'Rida', 20, 250)</code>. What would <code>print(<wbr>s.<wbr>name)</code> do?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>Student</code> has its own <code>__init__</code>, so Python runs only that one, and it stores just <code>self.<wbr>marks = 250</code>.</li>\n<li>The <code>__init__</code> of <code>Person</code> never runs, so <code>name</code> and <code>age</code> are never stored. (This is also why that run printed only <code>250</code> and <code>50000</code>.)</li>\n<li><code>s.name</code> asks for an attribute that the object does not have.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the program stops with <code>AttributeError: 'Student' object has no attribute 'name'</code>. The line <code>super().<wbr>__init__(<wbr>name, age)</code> is what fixes it.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">After the overriding change in 9.4, which methods run for <code>e = Employee(<wbr>'Harsh', 30, 50000)</code> and <code>e.display()</code>, and what is printed?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>Employee(<wbr>'Harsh', 30, 50000)</code> runs the <code>__init__</code> of <code>Employee</code>. Its first line, <code>super().<wbr>__init__(<wbr>name, age)</code>, runs the <code>__init__</code> of <code>Person</code>, which stores the name and the age. Then <code>self.<wbr>salary = 50000</code>.</li>\n<li><code>e.display()</code> runs the <code>display</code> of <code>Employee</code>, which overrides the one of <code>Person</code>: the <code>display</code> of <code>Person</code> does not run at all.</li>\n<li>That method prints all three values with one <code>print</code>, so they come on one line.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>Harsh 30 50000</code>. Overriding <code>display</code> does not change <code>__init__</code>: each method is inherited or overridden on its own.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The lecture’s homework (9.3): what are <code>int</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>list</code> and <code>dict</code>, and why does <code>type()</code> always say <code>class</code>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>They are <strong>classes</strong>, built into Python. <code>int()</code> calls the constructor of the class <code>int</code>, exactly as <code>Student()</code> calls the constructor of <code>Student</code>, and builds a new object: <code>int()</code> gives <code>0</code>, <code>str()</code> the empty string <code>''</code>, <code>list()</code> the empty list <code>[]</code> and <code>dict()</code> the empty dictionary <code>{}</code>.</li>\n<li><code>type(x)</code> gives the class of the object <code>x</code>, so <code>type(i)</code> is the class <code>int</code>, printed as <code>&lt;class 'int'&gt;</code>. Our own check: with <code>s0</code> from 9.3, <code>print(<wbr>type(s0))</code> prints <code>&lt;class '__main__.<wbr>Student'&gt;</code> (<code>__main__</code> is Python’s name for the file being run).</li>\n<li>Every value in Python is an object of some class: <code>5</code> is an object of the class <code>int</code>, <code>'abc'</code> of the class <code>str</code>. That is why the string functions are methods: <code>'abc'.<wbr>upper()</code> is short for <code>str.<wbr>upper(<wbr>'abc')</code>, just as <code>s0.display()</code> is short for <code>Student.<wbr>display(s0)</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>int</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>list</code> and <code>dict</code> are classes, their values are objects of those classes, and <code>type()</code> names the class. The <code>&lt;class 'int'&gt;</code> of <a href=\"#/python/w01/data-types\">Week 1</a> meant exactly that.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
